WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.668 --> 00:00:04.270 line:0%
-<i> ( music playing ) </i>
- Dan:<i> We're here for
 the fun silly wave.</i>

00:00:04.303 --> 00:00:05.271 align:start position:0% line:0%
That's correct, yes.

00:00:05.304 --> 00:00:06.606 align:start size:50% position:50% line:0%
Okay, all right.

00:00:06.639 --> 00:00:07.774 line:0%
Gav:<i> It looks like
 someone's just</i>

00:00:07.807 --> 00:00:09.709
<i> pulling on space time.</i>

00:00:09.742 --> 00:00:11.244 align:start size:84% position:16%
- ( grunts )
- Not really sure what
  I'm looking at.

00:00:11.277 --> 00:00:12.479
Dan:<i> Bath time.</i>

00:00:14.080 --> 00:00:15.048
Gav:<i> Those ducks
 just went--</i>

00:00:15.081 --> 00:00:16.382
Dan:<i> Whoosh!</i>

00:00:16.415 --> 00:00:18.251
Gav:<i> How do you feel
 about taking it</i>

00:00:18.284 --> 00:00:19.452 align:start position:0%
right in 
the face?

00:00:19.485 --> 00:00:20.586
Gav:<i> Dan, are
 you nervous?</i>

00:00:20.619 --> 00:00:22.055 align:start size:84% position:16%
- Oh.
- ( water splashing )

00:00:22.088 --> 00:00:25.325 align:start size:88% position:13%
- Gav:<i> ( chuckles ) Oh! </i>
- ( laughter )

00:00:29.495 --> 00:00:30.530 align:start position:0% line:0%
Well, that was
a very unique

00:00:30.563 --> 00:00:32.331 align:start position:0% line:0%
building we had
access to there.

00:00:32.364 --> 00:00:33.332 align:start size:66% position:34% line:0%
I love the fact 
that they just let us

00:00:33.365 --> 00:00:34.801 align:start size:63% position:38% line:0%
mess around with it.

00:00:34.834 --> 00:00:36.769 align:start size:69% position:31%
It's this incredibly
scientific and precise

00:00:36.802 --> 00:00:38.604 align:start size:66% position:34%
equipment and they
were like, "Go wild."

00:00:38.637 --> 00:00:40.139 align:start position:0%
Your life jacket
going off,

00:00:40.172 --> 00:00:41.541 align:start position:0%
unexpectedly,
almost wet myself.

00:00:41.574 --> 00:00:43.076 align:start size:53% position:47%
I think I even
said, I was like,

00:00:43.109 --> 00:00:44.077 align:start size:84% position:16%
- ( chuckles )
- "Huh, I'm surprised

00:00:44.110 --> 00:00:45.344 align:start size:69% position:31%
this hasn't gone off."

00:00:45.377 --> 00:00:46.712 align:start size:81% position:19%
and then about two seconds
later it--

00:00:46.745 --> 00:00:48.448 align:start size:81% position:19%
- ( air whooshing )
- Both: Whoa!

00:00:48.481 --> 00:00:51.250
Both: ( laughing )

00:00:51.283 --> 00:00:52.418 align:start position:0%
I think they're 
activated by salt

00:00:52.451 --> 00:00:53.719 align:start position:0%
aren't they? Like
it dissolves and

00:00:53.752 --> 00:00:55.354 align:start size:88% position:13%
- then it activates--
- Yes, the salt in there

00:00:55.387 --> 00:00:56.823 align:start size:44% position:56%
to make salt
crystal, yeah.

00:00:56.856 --> 00:00:58.424 align:start position:0%
Did it hurt taking
the spike wave in 
the mouth?

00:00:58.457 --> 00:00:59.725 align:start size:84% position:16%
- ( water splashing ) 
- Ooh!

00:00:59.758 --> 00:01:00.726
Dan:<i> I thought
 it was going to,</i>

00:01:00.759 --> 00:01:01.794 align:start size:72% position:28%
but it actually didn't.

00:01:01.827 --> 00:01:03.529
I really liked 
that shot where

00:01:03.562 --> 00:01:05.264
-<i> ( music playing ) </i>
-<i> it sort of tunnels</i>

00:01:05.297 --> 00:01:06.432
<i> around your face,
 you look like you're</i>

00:01:06.465 --> 00:01:08.734
<i> in a weird room that's moving.</i>

00:01:08.767 --> 00:01:10.703
Dan:<i> Totally surreal.
 No one had done</i>

00:01:10.736 --> 00:01:12.371
<i> that before, it was
 quite an honor really</i>

00:01:12.404 --> 00:01:13.573
<i> to be the first
 smashed in the face with it.</i>

00:01:13.606 --> 00:01:14.674
Gav:<i> Yeah.</i>

00:01:14.707 --> 00:01:16.142 align:start position:0%
So I think it'd 
be a good idea

00:01:16.175 --> 00:01:18.444 align:start position:0%
if we learned more
about waves in general,

00:01:18.477 --> 00:01:20.847 align:start position:0%
but also learn a little
bit more about the facility.

00:01:20.880 --> 00:01:23.149 align:start size:56% position:44%
So we had a little
chat with Tom.

00:01:23.182 --> 00:01:25.151 line:0%
Dr. Thomas Davey,
you're the Senior
Experimental Officer here.

00:01:25.184 --> 00:01:26.419 align:start size:66% position:34% line:0%
Yeah, that's correct,
yeah.

00:01:26.452 --> 00:01:28.354 line:0%
Okay, so, you can tell us
all about

00:01:28.387 --> 00:01:30.323 line:0%
what you do here essentially.

00:01:30.356 --> 00:01:33.459 align:start size:81% position:19% line:0%
Yes, so this is a facility
which essentially is

00:01:33.492 --> 00:01:36.329 line:0%
<i> just a model sea,
 about 20th scale.</i>

00:01:36.362 --> 00:01:38.397 line:0%
<i> And we we use that for
 lots of applications,</i>

00:01:38.430 --> 00:01:39.899 align:start size:63% position:38%
testing things like
wave energy devices,

00:01:39.932 --> 00:01:41.601
<i> tidal energy devices.</i>

00:01:41.634 --> 00:01:43.503
<i> Basically anything which
 we put in the sea.</i>

00:01:43.536 --> 00:01:46.506 size:88% position:56%
Okay, so if you make a small
wave about this big in here,

00:01:46.539 --> 00:01:48.441
you imagine it's
20 times the size.

00:01:48.474 --> 00:01:50.910
That's what you're imitating
essentially in the ocean.

00:01:50.943 --> 00:01:52.678 align:start size:97% position:3%
- Dr. Davey: <i> Exactly. </i>
- Dan:<i> So how long did it take
 to fill that thing?</i>

00:01:52.711 --> 00:01:54.780 align:start size:56% position:44%
Uh, it did take us
three weeks, so--

00:01:54.813 --> 00:01:57.150 align:start size:91% position:9%
- Three weeks?
- ...we don't like taking
  the water out of it.

00:01:57.183 --> 00:01:59.318
<i> It's two and a half thousand
 tons of water in this tank.</i>

00:01:59.351 --> 00:02:01.487 align:start position:0%
And what was the choice
behind making the tank

00:02:01.520 --> 00:02:02.855 align:start position:0%
this specific size?

00:02:02.888 --> 00:02:04.323 align:start size:69% position:31%
If you go much bigger,

00:02:04.356 --> 00:02:05.558 align:start size:59% position:41%
things start to get
very expensive.

00:02:05.591 --> 00:02:06.859 align:start size:69% position:31%
If you go much smaller
the scaling

00:02:06.892 --> 00:02:08.794 align:start size:56% position:44%
will start to work
against you, uh...

00:02:08.827 --> 00:02:10.363 align:start size:94% position:6%
- I imagine this was cheap,
  then.
- ( laughs )

00:02:10.396 --> 00:02:11.797 align:start size:47% position:53%
Yeah, so you're
looking at

00:02:11.830 --> 00:02:12.798
<i> about 6 millions pounds
 of investment</i>

00:02:12.831 --> 00:02:14.600
<i> just to build the tank.</i>

00:02:14.633 --> 00:02:15.868
<i> And actually the whole
 project's probably around</i>

00:02:15.901 --> 00:02:17.270
<i> 12 to 15 million.</i>

00:02:17.303 --> 00:02:18.905 align:start size:75% position:25%
Might be one of the more
expensive things

00:02:18.938 --> 00:02:20.506 align:start size:97% position:3%
- we've messed around with...
- ( laughs ) Yeah.

00:02:20.539 --> 00:02:21.741
...if I'm honest.
Cool.

00:02:21.774 --> 00:02:23.376 align:start size:53% position:47%
So how do you get
the waves to do

00:02:23.409 --> 00:02:24.777 align:start size:53% position:47%
the exact thing
you want them to?

00:02:24.810 --> 00:02:28.581 align:start size:69% position:31%
Well, this is the only
large circular wave

00:02:28.614 --> 00:02:30.550 align:start size:44% position:56%
and tidal tank
in the world.

00:02:30.583 --> 00:02:33.786
<i> So what we have is
 168 wave makers around
 the edge.</i>

00:02:33.819 --> 00:02:35.755
<i> These are basically paddles.</i>

00:02:35.788 --> 00:02:37.323
<i> So each individual paddle
on its own can generate a wave.</i>

00:02:37.356 --> 00:02:39.559
<i> But because we're in a circle,</i>

00:02:39.592 --> 00:02:41.394
<i> you can imagine, you can
 actually then generate
 from any direction,</i>

00:02:41.427 --> 00:02:43.362
<i> so you can have this one wave
 going in one direction,</i>

00:02:43.395 --> 00:02:45.531
<i>you can have thousands of waves
 going from all directions.</i>

00:02:45.564 --> 00:02:46.933 align:start size:72% position:28%
It's a bit like playing
a musical instrument.

00:02:46.966 --> 00:02:48.601
<i> You just combine all these
 notes together</i>

00:02:48.634 --> 00:02:51.470
<i> to create a very complex sea
 in the tank.</i>

00:02:51.503 --> 00:02:52.572 align:start size:75% position:25%
- Whoa-ho-ho-ho!
- Whoa!

00:02:52.605 --> 00:02:54.207
( water splashing )

00:02:54.240 --> 00:02:55.908 align:start size:88% position:13%
And then on top of that
you then put tidal currents.

00:02:55.941 --> 00:02:57.977
<i> So you make currents
 as well underneath...</i>

00:02:58.010 --> 00:02:59.912
Dr. Davey:
<i> Yes, yes. It works a bit
 like a conveyer belt.</i>

00:02:59.945 --> 00:03:01.380 align:start size:69% position:31%
The current comes from
underneath the tank,

00:03:01.413 --> 00:03:02.515 align:start size:69% position:31%
over the top,
and back around again.

00:03:02.548 --> 00:03:03.950 align:start size:69% position:31%
- Oh, okay.
- So...

00:03:03.983 --> 00:03:06.419
You said you can replicate
any part of the sea.

00:03:06.452 --> 00:03:09.222 align:start size:59% position:41%
If I said to you,
I want to replicate
Brighton.

00:03:09.255 --> 00:03:10.423 align:start position:0%
I want Brighton,
could you do that for me?

00:03:10.456 --> 00:03:11.691 align:start size:56% position:44%
As long as you got
the data.

00:03:11.724 --> 00:03:13.526
If we could get good data
for a site

00:03:13.559 --> 00:03:16.229 align:start size:72% position:28%
and we can understand
the energy of that site

00:03:16.262 --> 00:03:18.464 align:start size:72% position:28%
and the directions
of the waves and so on,
then yes.

00:03:18.497 --> 00:03:20.666 align:start position:0%
That way, if you just
brought in a load of sand,

00:03:20.699 --> 00:03:22.335 align:start position:0%
and people didn't fancy the,
like, eight hour drive,

00:03:22.368 --> 00:03:24.437 align:start position:0%
you could have people
just come in here

00:03:24.470 --> 00:03:26.339 align:start size:97% position:3%
- and enjoy the--
- Yeah, we'd be in the surfing
  business.

00:03:26.372 --> 00:03:27.673
( laughter )

00:03:27.706 --> 00:03:29.508
Thanks, Tom,
that was really interesting

00:03:29.541 --> 00:03:32.278
and thanks for letting us use
your very expensive pool.

00:03:32.311 --> 00:03:33.279
Dr. Davey: No problem.

00:03:33.312 --> 00:03:34.814 align:start position:0%
Back to you,
Gav and Dan.

00:03:34.847 --> 00:03:36.315 align:start position:0%
Thanks, us.

00:03:36.348 --> 00:03:37.783 align:start position:0%
Why don't we learn
some more about waves.

00:03:37.816 --> 00:03:38.951 align:start size:59% position:41%
Yeah, we've invited
someone along

00:03:38.984 --> 00:03:40.553 align:start size:47% position:53%
who's travelled
the world,

00:03:40.586 --> 00:03:43.022 align:start size:66% position:34%
and written a book
about all the world's

00:03:43.055 --> 00:03:44.624 align:start size:56% position:44%
scariest, largest,
fastest waves.

00:03:44.657 --> 00:03:45.858 align:start size:63% position:38%
It's called "Tides."

00:03:45.891 --> 00:03:46.859 align:start size:47% position:53%
Hi, Jonathan.
How's it going?

00:03:46.892 --> 00:03:48.694 align:start size:66% position:34% line:0%
- Hi, Dan.
- Hey.

00:03:48.727 --> 00:03:52.865 align:start position:0% line:0%
Could you explain to us,
the difference between waves
and tides?

00:03:52.898 --> 00:03:54.867
Sure, waves, as we
talked about

00:03:54.900 --> 00:03:58.337
are generally created
in the storm, right?

00:03:58.370 --> 00:04:00.273
There's a one pulse
that makes the wave

00:04:00.306 --> 00:04:01.974
and then the waves
travel away from that.

00:04:02.007 --> 00:04:05.811
But a tide is actually
also a wave,

00:04:05.844 --> 00:04:08.581
but it's the largest wave
on the planet.

00:04:08.614 --> 00:04:10.916
<i> And it's a long low wave</i>

00:04:10.949 --> 00:04:14.754
<i> that travels about
 450 miles per hour
 around the globe.</i>

00:04:14.787 --> 00:04:17.290
We don't experience it
as a fast moving phenomenon

00:04:17.323 --> 00:04:19.992
because if we're on the coast,
we have to stay there all day

00:04:20.025 --> 00:04:21.494
to watch it pass, right?

00:04:21.527 --> 00:04:24.063 align:start size:63% position:38%
Six hours of trough,
which is low tide,

00:04:24.096 --> 00:04:27.767 align:start size:84% position:16%
and then about
six hours later, the crest,
high tide.

00:04:27.800 --> 00:04:29.869 align:start position:0%
So you could potentially
be on a plane,

00:04:29.902 --> 00:04:32.605 align:start position:0%
unknowingly flying
at the same speed

00:04:32.638 --> 00:04:34.607 align:start size:94% position:6%
- as a tide rolling through.
- That's right.

00:04:34.640 --> 00:04:36.842
<i> The oceans actually
 aren't deep enough</i>

00:04:36.875 --> 00:04:39.679
<i> for this free wave,
 this wave of tide,
 to travel</i>

00:04:39.712 --> 00:04:41.547
<i> as fast as it wants
 to travel.</i>

00:04:41.580 --> 00:04:43.516
<i> So essentially it drags
 its legs</i>

00:04:43.549 --> 00:04:44.850
<i> on the bottom of the ocean,</i>

00:04:44.883 --> 00:04:46.919
<i> and slows down
 and creates friction.</i>

00:04:46.952 --> 00:04:48.721 align:start position:0%
Friction against
the entire Earth?

00:04:48.754 --> 00:04:50.089
- That's right.
- Wow, yeah.

00:04:50.122 --> 00:04:51.991 align:start position:0%
'Cause I've heard that
they occasionally

00:04:52.024 --> 00:04:55.328 align:start position:0%
will add like a leap second
to the clocks

00:04:55.361 --> 00:04:57.330 align:start position:0%
to compensate for
the Earth slowing down.

00:04:57.363 --> 00:05:00.066
Tides have literally
slowed down the rotation
of the Earth.

00:05:00.099 --> 00:05:01.734
Acted as a break.

00:05:01.767 --> 00:05:04.637 align:start size:69% position:31%
'Cause the tides are
slowing down the world

00:05:04.670 --> 00:05:06.539 align:start size:78% position:22%
by friction on the bottom
of the sea.

00:05:06.572 --> 00:05:08.407 align:start size:78% position:22%
That means every millions
of years,

00:05:08.440 --> 00:05:11.010 align:start size:59% position:41%
there's changes to
the amount of hours
in a day?

00:05:11.043 --> 00:05:13.112
Yeah, about 400 million
years ago,

00:05:13.145 --> 00:05:16.449
our day 21 hours long,
not 24.

00:05:16.482 --> 00:05:17.683
Because of the tide.

00:05:17.716 --> 00:05:19.485 align:start position:0%
I'm okay with it,
because I feel like

00:05:19.518 --> 00:05:21.454 align:start position:0%
I'd struggle to fit
everything into
a 21 hour day.

00:05:21.487 --> 00:05:22.755 align:start size:75% position:25%
Yeah, so you're thinking
that millions of years

00:05:22.788 --> 00:05:24.123 align:start size:75% position:25%
when it's a 30 hour day,

00:05:24.156 --> 00:05:25.591 align:start size:97% position:3%
- people are gonna be like...
- ( laughs )

00:05:25.624 --> 00:05:27.626 align:start size:44% position:56%
( sputters )
...a long day.

00:05:27.659 --> 00:05:30.096 size:78% position:39%
Yeah, the days got longer
and then people started
having longer meetings.

00:05:30.129 --> 00:05:31.097 align:start size:88% position:13%
- ( laughs )
- That's what happened.

00:05:31.130 --> 00:05:32.898
Yeah.

00:05:32.931 --> 00:05:35.134
<i> So when we filmed
 the footage in the wave pool,</i>

00:05:35.167 --> 00:05:37.703
<i> it looked a bit like
 a sound wave in 3D space.</i>

00:05:37.736 --> 00:05:40.072
<i> And you've done experiments
 before with vibrations
 and frequency</i>

00:05:40.105 --> 00:05:41.874
to try and show
what's happening with the tides

00:05:41.907 --> 00:05:44.844
and you've asked us
to get this stuff here.

00:05:44.877 --> 00:05:46.379 align:start size:75% position:25%
Yes, this is the Chladni
experiment

00:05:46.412 --> 00:05:48.414 align:start size:81% position:19%
and uh, that little device
over there

00:05:48.447 --> 00:05:50.616 align:start size:66% position:34%
is a sign wave signal
generator.

00:05:50.649 --> 00:05:53.018 align:start size:81% position:19%
And it's gonna function
like the sun and the moon.

00:05:53.051 --> 00:05:55.421 align:start size:66% position:34%
The vibrations from
the sun and the moon.

00:05:55.454 --> 00:05:57.757 align:start size:59% position:41%
And this area here,
this is the ocean.

00:05:57.790 --> 00:05:59.492 align:start size:75% position:25%
and we're gonna put some
salt on that

00:05:59.525 --> 00:06:01.093 align:start size:81% position:19%
and it's gonna demonstrate
how the salt

00:06:01.126 --> 00:06:03.729 align:start size:53% position:47%
will dance around
or resonate with

00:06:03.762 --> 00:06:05.931 align:start size:81% position:19%
the various signals
from the sun and the moon.

00:06:05.964 --> 00:06:07.867 align:start size:59% position:41%
When scientist look
at tides,

00:06:07.900 --> 00:06:10.936 align:start size:66% position:34%
it's really all about
the oceans responding

00:06:10.969 --> 00:06:13.038 align:start size:59% position:41%
to the vibrations,
or pulses, or beats

00:06:13.071 --> 00:06:14.774 align:start size:41% position:59%
from the sun
and the moon.

00:06:14.807 --> 00:06:17.176
So what I've got here
is a way to look at that.

00:06:17.209 --> 00:06:19.478
So go ahead and pour
the salt on there...

00:06:19.511 --> 00:06:21.447 align:start size:91% position:9%
- Okay.
- ...and as you turn
  the signal generator on,

00:06:21.480 --> 00:06:22.615 align:start position:0%
You wanna do it?

00:06:22.648 --> 00:06:23.749 align:start size:63% position:38%
Well, you're the sun
and the moon.

00:06:23.782 --> 00:06:25.084 align:start size:72% position:28%
- That's fair.
- Yeah.

00:06:25.117 --> 00:06:27.086
Jonathan: So as you turn
the signal generator on

00:06:27.119 --> 00:06:28.988
you'll start to see patterns
form here.

00:06:29.021 --> 00:06:30.656
Gav: Why don't we start
with 60?

00:06:30.689 --> 00:06:32.191
Oh, I see a bit moving
around the edges.

00:06:32.224 --> 00:06:33.526
Dan: Gibberin'.

00:06:33.559 --> 00:06:35.561
- It's gibbering
- I like that.

00:06:35.594 --> 00:06:37.830
Whoa.

00:06:37.863 --> 00:06:39.498 align:start size:94% position:6%
- Jonathan: Oh, wow,
  it's bouncing.
- Dan: Oh, wow. That's cool.

00:06:39.531 --> 00:06:41.066
Gav: An "X."
Get more salt on there.

00:06:41.099 --> 00:06:42.635
Sure, let's get that
on there.

00:06:42.668 --> 00:06:43.836 align:start position:0%
None of that salt
wants to be in the middle,

00:06:43.869 --> 00:06:45.438
that's interesting.

00:06:45.471 --> 00:06:47.106 align:start position:0%
- Dan: Oh, that's really weird.
- ( Gav laughs )

00:06:47.139 --> 00:06:48.741
Dan: It looks like static
from a TV.

00:06:48.774 --> 00:06:50.509 align:start position:0%
Oh, it's spreading.
Circle's getting bigger.

00:06:50.542 --> 00:06:51.811
Do it again.

00:06:53.545 --> 00:06:55.748 align:start size:69% position:31%
- Oh.
- Gav: Yeah.

00:06:55.781 --> 00:06:57.016 align:start size:59% position:41%
